{
  "abstract": "A double cone is obtained by adding two adjacent universal vertices to a graph called its body. We prove that every biplanar double cone on at most twenty-two vertices is nine-colorable, with no restriction on the independence number. At twenty-three vertices, a ten-chromatic example must have body independence number exactly four and at least eighty-one of the eighty-three possible body edges allowed by the two planar layers. At eighty-one edges some maximum independent four-set must leave a seven-critical subgraph on thirteen vertices. The order proof deletes a maximum independent set and uses both its domination of the critical subgraph and the colors available at its individual vertices. For the final twenty-vertex body, the critical subgraph has thirteen vertices and the seven remaining vertices inherit lists from one coloring of the subgraph. We verify nonzero signed graph-polynomial coefficients for all 178 surviving labeled boundary choices. At the next order we classify the overlaps of copies of the join of a four-clique and a pentagon in a critical body. Exchanging a shared vertex into a maximum independent set forces more edges than a coloring obstruction can afford. The supplement provides the hand reductions, signed coefficients, and standard-library programs for regenerating all degree tables.",
